diff --git a/docs/mobile-push-integration.md b/docs/mobile-push-integration.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8171baf
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/mobile-push-integration.md
@@ -0,0 +1,78 @@
+# Mobile Android phone alerts (push)
+
+Apply `mobile-push-notifications.patch` after `mobile-notification-preferences.patch` and the preceding version-4 mobile patches on clean backend `42b4552`. Then run Prisma generate and migrate deploy before restarting the API. The first additive migration creates `beneficiary_mobile_push_devices` and `beneficiary_mobile_push_deliveries`; the second versions registrations and snapshots the registration used for a send. An existing installation that already applied the first migration applies only the second one; no reset is needed. No dependency or staff frontend change is added. Sending uses Node's built-in `fetch` against Expo's push service.
+
+## What it does
+
+- A signed-in beneficiary can turn on phone alerts for **the current mobile session** only. This is a separate opt-in from the saved category preferences.
+- When a staff transaction creates an automatic in-app notice through `createMobileNotifications`, the same transaction writes one outbox row per eligible phone. If the staff transaction rolls back, nothing is queued.
+- An in-process dispatcher (`src/workers/mobilePush.worker.js`) polls the committed outbox. It claims rows with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` and calls Expo **outside any database transaction**. It then records each result only if it still owns the claim.
+- Lock-screen text is generic in English or Bisaya ("You have a new account update. Open the app to view it."). The payload carries only `{ kind: "garantiyaid.notification", notificationId }`, and the app loads the details through the signed-in account.
+
+## Routes (mobile session required, `Cache-Control: private, no-store`)
+
+- `GET /api/v1/mobile/beneficiaries/me/push-device` returns `{ configured, status: "unavailable" | "off" | "on" | "invalid", enabled, language, updatedAt }`. It never returns the token.
+- `PUT /api/v1/mobile/beneficiaries/me/push-device` with body `{ token: "ExponentPushToken[...]", platform: "android", language: "en" | "bs" }`. The token travels only in this JSON body, never in a URL. The route is strictly validated and rate-limited with the mobile authentication limiter. It returns 503 `PUSH_UNAVAILABLE` when push is not configured.
+- `DELETE /api/v1/mobile/beneficiaries/me/push-device` turns alerts off for this session.
+
+## Security and delivery contract
+
+- **Token storage.** Tokens are AES-256-GCM encrypted with `FIELD_ENCRYPTION_KEY`, bound to their device row. A keyed HMAC (`MOBILE_AUTH_SECRET`) enforces one owner per physical device. Raw tokens are never logged, stored in plain text, returned, or placed in URLs. Provider error text, which can echo tokens, is reduced to known-shaped codes.
+- **Account changes.** Registering a token that belongs to another session moves it: the previous session or account stops receiving alerts in the same commit, and its pending alerts cascade away. Logout deletes the session's registration in the logout transaction.
+- **Send-time re-check.** Before sending, the dispatcher re-checks the following. Anything ineligible is cancelled with a reason code.
+  - The session is active and not expired, and its auth mode matches.
+  - The account is verified and active.
+  - The device belongs to the notice owner.
+  - The notice is a visible IN_APP notice that hasn't already been read.
+  - The category preference is still enabled.
+  - The alert is less than 24 hours old.
+- **Duplicates.**
+  - The outbox has a unique `(notification_id, device_id)` key.
+  - Staff replays that hit an existing notice deduplication key queue nothing.
+  - A successful or unconfirmed send is not deliberately resent. A provider-confirmed rate-limit rejection may return just that alert to the pending queue.
+  - A row whose send outcome is unknown (in flight past the 2-minute lease, or a provider timeout) is marked `FAILED` with `PUSH_RESULT_UNKNOWN`/`PUSH_PROVIDER_TIMEOUT` rather than resent. Expo/provider delivery itself remains best effort, not an exactly-once guarantee.
+- **Retries.** Network failures, 429/5xx and individual `MessageRateExceeded` ticket/receipt errors back off (30 s, then 60 s) for up to three send attempts. A rejected receipt clears the old ticket and receipt state before requeueing only its alert. Other 4xx responses and permanent ticket/receipt errors finish without resending. Request deadlines cover response bodies as well as headers.
+- **Invalid tokens.** Each registration receives a fresh internal registration ID. Sends snapshot it; `DeviceNotRegistered` disables only that matching registration and cancels only its pending alerts. Delayed results for an older registration cannot invalidate a replacement or cancel its alerts. Pre-upgrade sent rows have no snapshot and conservatively cannot disable a current registration. GET reports `invalid` only for a disabled current registration, and the app asks the user to turn alerts on again. Receipts are checked about 15 minutes after sending; Expo keeps them for 24 hours.
+- **Housekeeping.** About once a minute, unknown in-flight rows are failed, registrations of revoked or expired sessions are deleted, and finished delivery history older than 30 days is removed.
+
+## Configuration
+
+```
+MOBILE_PUSH_MODE=EXPO                  # default DISABLED; EXPO requires MOBILE_AUTH_SECRET and FIELD_ENCRYPTION_KEY
+# MOBILE_PUSH_EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN=       # only if Enhanced push security is enabled for the Expo project
+MOBILE_PUSH_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
+MOBILE_PUSH_DISPATCH_INTERVAL_MS=3000
+```
+
+The dispatcher starts with the API only in `EXPO` mode. With `DISABLED`, no outbox rows are written, registration returns 503, and the app shows "not set up on this server". Running several API instances is safe because claims use database row locks. Redis and the existing BullMQ notification worker are not required.
+
+Real delivery also needs:
+- an Expo (EAS) project ID in the mobile app config;
+- an FCM V1 service-account key uploaded to that project's credentials;
+- the matching `google-services.json` and Android package in a development or release build. Expo Go cannot receive Android remote push since SDK 53.
+
+## Tests
+
+- `test/mobile-push.test.js` (default suite) covers:
+  - provider request shape, access token, retry classification and token-free errors;
+  - a response body stalling after headers, for sends and receipt lookups;
+  - receipts;
+  - generic EN/BS messages;
+  - token encryption and binding;
+  - strict input;
+  - disabled mode.
+- `npm run test:mobile:push` (explicit, isolated database only, `RUN_MOBILE_DB_TESTS=1`) uses a fake provider to cover:
+  - per-session opt-in and privacy;
+  - rollback and replay safety;
+  - a single send per notice;
+  - preference and read-state checks at send time;
+  - backoff and the attempt limit;
+  - individual rate limits from tickets and receipts, stale registration results and pre-upgrade sends;
+  - at-most-once handling of unknown outcomes;
+  - `DeviceNotRegistered` from tickets and receipts, and re-enabling;
+  - moving a device between accounts;
+  - turning alerts off, logout, and session expiry;
+  - disabled mode;
+  - no tokens in logs.
+
+None of these tests sends a real push notification. Delivery to a phone must be validated on a device.
